### Would you split a 300K-line service into microservices?

Not because of the line count. Size tells you the service is big; it does not tell you where the boundaries are or whether a network boundary would help. The decision must follow evidence.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class SplitDecision {
    public static String evaluate(boolean changesIndependently,
                                  boolean hasOwner,
                                  boolean needsIndependentScaling,
                                  boolean failureAffectsOthers) {
        if (changesIndependently && hasOwner
                && (needsIndependentScaling || failureAffectsOthers)) {
            return "EXTRACT: proven independent need exists";
        }
        return "MODULAR MONOLITH: strengthen internal boundaries first";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, true, true, true));   // extract
        System.out.println(evaluate(true, false, true, false)); // no owner
        System.out.println(evaluate(false, true, false, false)); // co-changes
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
EXTRACT: proven independent need exists
MODULAR MONOLITH: strengthen internal boundaries first
MODULAR MONOLITH: strengthen internal boundaries first
```

### How do you measure change coupling?

Change coupling shows which files always change together in the same commits. If transfers and accounts always co-change, splitting them spreads one logical change across two deployables.

```bash
# Measure which files co-change in the same commits
git log --format=format: --name-only | sort | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -20
```

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class ChangeCouplingAnalysis {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // If transfers/ and accounts/ co-change in 80% of commits:
        int totalCommits = 500;
        int coChangeCommits = 400;
        double coupling = (double) coChangeCommits / totalCommits;
        System.out.printf("Change coupling: %.0f%%%n", coupling * 100);
        if (coupling > 0.5) {
            System.out.println("HIGH coupling: splitting these would spread changes");
            System.out.println("across two deployables. Keep together.");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Change coupling: 80%
HIGH coupling: splitting these would spread changes
across two deployables. Keep together.
```

### How do you evaluate ownership?

A split pays off when it aligns with a single team that owns the service end to end: code, deployment, data, on-call. If no team would clearly own the extracted service, the split creates an orphan.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class OwnershipCheck {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String module = "fraud-scoring";
        boolean hasDedicatedTeam = true;
        boolean teamOwnsOnCall   = true;
        boolean teamOwnsData     = true;

        if (hasDedicatedTeam && teamOwnsOnCall && teamOwnsData) {
            System.out.println(module + ": clear ownership. Split is viable.");
        } else {
            System.out.println(module + ": no clear owner. Split creates an orphan.");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
fraud-scoring: clear ownership. Split is viable.
```

### How do you evaluate blast radius?

If a runaway report generation exhausts the heap and takes down transfers, that is real fault coupling a split could fix. But if capabilities are already isolated by threading or resource limits, a split buys little.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class BlastRadiusCheck {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Scenario: report generation causes OOM, takes down transfer processing
        boolean reportOomAffectsTransfers = true;
        if (reportOomAffectsTransfers) {
            System.out.println("Blast radius: report failure kills transfers.");
            System.out.println("Splitting report-generation isolates the fault.");
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Blast radius: report failure kills transfers.
Splitting report-generation isolates the fault.
```

### What is the microservices tax?

Every network boundary imports a permanent tax. This tax is paid on every change and every incident, not once.

| In-Process (Monolith) | Cross-Service (Microservices) |
|---|---|
| Method call | Network call (slow, can fail, can timeout) |
| Local transaction | Saga or 2PC |
| Stack trace | Distributed tracing |
| Direct reference | Service discovery |
| One deploy | Coordinated or independent deploys |

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class MicroservicesTax {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Method call       --> Network call (latency, failure, timeout)");
        System.out.println("Local transaction --> Saga (eventual consistency)");
        System.out.println("Stack trace       --> Distributed tracing (correlation IDs)");
        System.out.println("Direct reference  --> Service discovery (registry lookup)");
        System.out.println("One deploy        --> Multiple pipelines to coordinate");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Method call       --> Network call (latency, failure, timeout)
Local transaction --> Saga (eventual consistency)
Stack trace       --> Distributed tracing (correlation IDs)
Direct reference  --> Service discovery (registry lookup)
One deploy        --> Multiple pipelines to coordinate
```

### What is the modular monolith alternative?

A modular monolith enforces boundaries inside one deployable. Each module exposes only a public interface; architecture tests prevent reaching into internals. This captures most of the organizational benefit with none of the distributed-systems tax.

```java
package com.bank.transfers;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// Public interface: the only way other modules interact with transfers
public interface TransferModule {
    TransferResult execute(String fromAccountId, String toAccountId, BigDecimal amount);
}
```

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

import com.tngtech.archunit.junit.ArchTest;
import com.tngtech.archunit.lang.ArchRule;
import static com.tngtech.archunit.lang.syntax.ArchRuleDefinition.noClasses;

public class ModuleBoundaryTest {
    // Prevent accounts module from reaching into transfers internals
    @ArchTest
    static final ArchRule moduleBoundary = noClasses()
        .that().resideInAPackage("..accounts..")
        .should().dependOnClassesThat().resideInAPackage("..transfers.internal..");
}
```

```text
Expected behavior:
ArchUnit test FAILS if accounts code imports transfers.internal classes.
Boundary enforced at compile time, not runtime.
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is splitting because 300K lines "feels too big," without evidence that the split solves a real problem.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class CommonMistakes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // WRONG: splitting because 300K lines "feels too big"
        // Now every feature change requires coordinating 5 services
        System.out.println("WRONG: split because big --> 5 services, 5 pipelines,");
        System.out.println("       5 on-call rotations for what was one module");

        // CORRECT: establish boundaries internally, measure coupling,
        // extract only the capability with a proven need
        System.out.println("CORRECT: modular monolith first, measure, extract only");
        System.out.println("         the module with proven independent need");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
WRONG: split because big --> 5 services, 5 pipelines,
       5 on-call rotations for what was one module
CORRECT: modular monolith first, measure, extract only
         the module with proven independent need
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

The mark of seniority is knowing when to merge back, not just when to split.

- Start by establishing boundaries internally with a modular monolith.
- Measure change coupling from version-control history before proposing a split.
- Extract only when there is a concrete need: independent deployment, scaling, fault isolation, or separate ownership.
- The extraction is cheap if the internal boundary already exists.
- If you cannot separate the data, the services are split along the wrong boundaries; consider merging back.

```java
package com.bank.architecture;

public class SeniorDecision {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Step 1: Enforce internal module boundaries (ArchUnit)");
        System.out.println("Step 2: Measure change coupling from git history");
        System.out.println("Step 3: Identify modules with independent owner + scaling need");
        System.out.println("Step 4: Extract ONLY that module, along the existing seam");
        System.out.println("Step 5: If data cannot separate cleanly, merge back");
    }
}
```

```text
Expected output:
Step 1: Enforce internal module boundaries (ArchUnit)
Step 2: Measure change coupling from git history
Step 3: Identify modules with independent owner + scaling need
Step 4: Extract ONLY that module, along the existing seam
Step 5: If data cannot separate cleanly, merge back
```
